A TIM 8 thermal camera from Micro-Epsilon was successfully integrated as part of the DED-Arc system to measure all relevant temperatures and cooling gradients, i.e., preheating and interpass temperatures as well as Δ t 8/5  cooling time. The calibratable measurement range of this camera is between −20 and 900 °C with an accuracy of ± 2 K. It uses an uncooled focal plane array (FPA) detector and operates within a spectral range of 7.5 µm to 13.5 µm. The measurements were carried out with a constant emission coefficient  ε  = 1. The raw thermographic measurement results were then calibrated, taking into account the build-up height, local temperature, surface color and texture according to Mueller et al. [ 25 ]. This enabled the in-situ analysis of selected temperature profiles within the video stream of the thermal camera. As part of this work, the layer-by-layer cooling time was recorded as a heat treatment benchmark for the final interpretation and discussion of the residual stress data. In contrast to the microstructurally focused analysis of the cooling time using Δ t 8/5 , the benchmark parameter Δ t X–450 °C  was introduced. The aim was to better represent the thermal load in temperature ranges outside the interval from 800 to 500 °C, which are also relevant for residual stress relaxation. 1 Δ t X - 450 ∘ C = t X - t 450 ∘ C \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
